---
import DocsPage from '../../components/DocsPage.astro'
import { sitePath } from '../../data/site-path'
---

<DocsPage
  title="Get a Solid grid running"
  eyebrow="INTEGRATION GUIDE"
  summary="Install the Solid binding, paste one file, and verify a real formula."
  alternateLocaleHref={sitePath('zh/docs/getting-started/')}
>
  <div class="docs-guide">
    <section class="docs-guide-callout docs-guide-callout-primary" aria-label="Who this guide is for">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">THE RESULT</p>
      <p>
        In five minutes, a Solid + Vite app renders an editable grid backed by a Worker and the Rust/WASM workbook
        engine. You will type <code>=1+2</code> and see <code>3</code> in the cell.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>Choose the route before installing</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-paths">
      <a class="docs-guide-path docs-guide-path-featured" href="#install">
        <span>FORMAL UI BINDING</span><strong>Solid</strong><small>This is the supported install-and-render path.</small>
      </a>
      <a class="docs-guide-path" href={sitePath('docs/react/')}>
        <span>REFERENCE</span><strong>React</strong><small>Use the controlled-projection demo, not a fictional adapter.</small>
      </a>
      <a class="docs-guide-path" href={sitePath('docs/vue/')}>
        <span>REFERENCE</span><strong>Vue</strong><small>Use the controlled-projection demo to define the host boundary.</small>
      </a>
      <a class="docs-guide-path" href={sitePath('docs/backend-port/')}>
        <span>CONTRACT</span><strong>Your own backend</strong><small>Start here when workbook data and compute already belong to your product.</small>
      </a>
    </div>

    <h2>Before you begin</h2>
    <ul class="docs-guide-checklist">
      <li>Node.js 22.12 or later.</li>
      <li>A Vite + Solid TypeScript application. Create one with <code>npm create vite@latest my-sheet -- --template solid-ts</code> if needed.</li>
      <li>An <code>index.html</code> containing <code>&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="install">1. Install the Solid integration</h2>
    <pre><code>{`npm install @einfach/solid-excel @einfach/core @einfach/solid solid-js`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The three non-UI packages are peers. Keep one physical copy of each in the application dependency graph; the
      provider and its consumers must share the same Solid runtime.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Paste one working application</h2>
    <p>Replace <code>src/main.tsx</code> with this example. It uses the bundled Worker backend, so it is the safest default for a first integration.</p>
    <pre><code>{`import { render } from 'solid-js/web'
import {
  createWorkerWorkbookSpreadsheetBackend,
  SpreadsheetGrid,
  SpreadsheetUiProvider,
} from '@einfach/solid-excel/vnext'
import { defaultVNextWorkbookWorkerFactory } from '@einfach/solid-excel/vnext-worker-factory'
import '@einfach/solid-excel/vnext-styles.css'

const backend = createWorkerWorkbookSpreadsheetBackend({
  workerFactory: defaultVNextWorkbookWorkerFactory,
  sheets: [{ id: 'sheet-1', name: 'Sheet1' }],
})

const viewport = {
  scrollTop: 0, scrollLeft: 0, viewportHeight: 480, viewportWidth: 960,
  rowHeight: 24, colWidth: 96, rowCount: 50, colCount: 12,
  overscanRows: 2, overscanCols: 1,
}

const root = document.getElementById('root')
if (!root) throw new Error('Missing #root')

render(() => (
  <SpreadsheetUiProvider backend={backend}>
    <SpreadsheetGrid sheetId="sheet-1" viewport={viewport} />
  </SpreadsheetUiProvider>
), root)`}</code></pre>

    <div class="docs-guide-terms" aria-label="What the example adds">
      <article><strong>Worker backend</strong><span>Owns workbook data and formula computation away from the main thread.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Provider</strong><span>Connects that backend to the UI state once at the application boundary.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Grid</strong><span>Requests only the projection that is visible inside the viewport.</span></article>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Verify the integration</h2>
    <ol class="docs-guide-steps">
      <li>Run <code>npm run dev</code>.</li>
      <li>Open the Vite URL, then double-click cell A1.</li>
      <li>Enter <code>=1+2</code> and press Enter. A1 should display <code>3</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If the grid renders and the formula resolves, the UI, provider, Worker transport, and workbook engine are connected.</p>

    <details class="docs-guide-details">
      <summary>Vite development note for the current 0.1.0 package</summary>
      <p>
        Vite 8 is the verified dev path. If the dev server fails while resolving the package's message-format dependency,
        add the following optimization to <code>vite.config.ts</code>; production build and preview do not need it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`optimizeDeps: {
  include: [
    '@einfach/solid-excel > @lingui/core',
    '@einfach/solid-excel > @lingui/core > @messageformat/parser',
  ],
}`}</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('demos/workbench/')}><strong>Build a complete workbench</strong><span>See toolbar, formula bar, sheets, comments, and selection working together. →</span></a>
      <a href={sitePath('docs/backend-port/')}><strong>Bring your own data model</strong><span>Implement the bounded backend contract instead of moving a full workbook into the browser. →</span></a>
      <a href={sitePath('docs/atoms/viewport/')}><strong>Understand viewport ownership</strong><span>Learn why viewport state stays separate from the authoritative workbook. →</span></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
